2026-09-16T12:55Z claude note Design constraint surfaced by the harness session at its 0906 merge, verified here against isCleanUpgrade (manifest.go:391) rather than reasoned about. The hard error must fire on an UNRECOGNIZED hash and must NOT fire on a stamp that is merely behind the running binary. These are different states and the machinery already tells them apart: with a stamp present, isCleanUpgrade tests diskHash == stampedHash and never consults the currently-embedded hash, so a store whose file matches its own stamp is a clean upgrade whatever the binary now ships; only a stampless store falls back to the known-hash list. Separately, managedAssetWarnings compares the STAMP against the embedded version and warns when the binary has moved past it -- that warning means 'a re-init would refresh you', it is the designed nudge, and turning it into an error would fail every adopter who has simply not re-inited since the last release. Worked case that prompted this: ImperialDragonHarness restored its AGENTS.md to the 2356-byte asset its vendored binary embeds, with a committed manifest; git-erg main now ships 2393. That repo must stay green, and under the rule above it does -- its file matches its stamp. If the enforcement had keyed on the embedded hash instead, it would have gone red on a file made pristine an hour earlier.
